To count how many cookies you've set and retrieve their values in your Mootools-based todo list application, you can follow these steps:

1. **Count Cookies**: JavaScript cookies can be accessed via `document.cookie`, which returns a single string containing all cookies. You can split this string by the `;` character to count how many cookies you have.

2. **Loop Through Cookies**: After splitting the cookie string, you can loop through the resulting array to access individual cookie values.

Here’s how you can modify your current code to achieve that:

```javascript
window.addEvent('domready', function() {
    $('add').addEvent('click', function() {
        var value = $('todo').value;
        var t = new Todo(value, "beschrijving", new Date(), 1);
        var storeData = JSON.encode(t);

        // Write the cookie
        Cookie.write(value, storeData, { duration: 1 });

        // Read all cookies and count them
        var allCookies = document.cookie.split(';');
        console.log("Total cookies: " + allCookies.length); // This will give you the count

        // Loop through cookies to find your values
        allCookies.forEach(function(cookie) {
            var cookiePair = cookie.trim().split('=');
            var cookieName = cookiePair[0];
            var cookieValue = cookiePair[1];

            console.log("Cookie Name: " + cookieName + ", Value: " + cookieValue);
        });

        // Check what was just written
        var getData = Cookie.read(value);
        console.log(getData);
    });
});

var Todo = new Class({
    initialize: function(title, description, date, isDone) {
        this.title = title;
        this.description = description;
        this.date = new Date();
        this.isDone = isDone;
    },

    getTitle: function() {       
        return this.title;  
    },

    getIsDone: function() {
        return this.isDone;
    },

    setIsDone: function(value) {
        this.isDone = value;
    }
});
```

### Key Points:
- The `document.cookie` property is used to get all the cookies as a single string.
- By splitting this string by `;`, you can count and access each cookie.
- Each cookie can be further split by `=` to separate the cookie name from its value.

By implementing these changes, you'll be able to count your cookies and retrieve their values effectively. If you want to enhance your todo list further, consider using localStorage instead of cookies, as it allows for easier data manipulation and storage without the complexity of dealing with cookie strings [SO-11470987].

